{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-4-6", "id": "msg_vrtx_018tCVStQiNXMg1vgV9Masy9", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_vrtx_01YKKcQg1foD32x1fquQ9uje", "name": "Edit", "input": {"replace_all": false, "file_path": "/private/var/folders/3v/16m6pn090hl4d60grm1057ww0000gn/T/agent-eval/2026-05-04-opus/cases/case-004-tutorial-with-code/source.md", "old_string": "## Setting Up A PostgreSQL Connection Pool\n\nIn order to establish a connection pool in your Go application, you'll need to leverage the `pgxpool` package. This groundbreaking library serves as a robust foundation for managing database connections efficiently.\n\nFirst, install the package:\n\n```bash\ngo get github.com/jackc/pgx/v5/pgxpool\n```\n\nNext, create the pool configuration. The following code showcases how to set up a connection pool with sensible defaults:\n\n```go\nconfig, err := pgxpool.ParseConfig(\"postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/mydb\")\nif err != nil {\n    log.Fatal(err)\n}\nconfig.MaxConns = 10\nconfig.MinConns = 2\n\npool, err := pgxpool.NewWithConfig(context.Background(), config)\nif err != nil {\n    log.Fatal(err)\n}\ndefer pool.Close()\n```\n\nIt is important to note that the `MaxConns` parameter plays a crucial role in determining the pool's behavior under load. Additionally, the `MinConns` setting ensures that a minimum number of connections are maintained, fostering improved response times for initial queries.\n\nThe pool boasts automatic connection health checking and reconnection capabilities. Due to the fact that connections can become stale, the pool periodically validates them, highlighting the intricate interplay between resource management and reliability.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you'd like me to delve into connection lifecycle management.", "new_string": "## Setting up a PostgreSQL connection pool\n\nThe `pgxpool` package manages connection pooling for Go applications using PostgreSQL.\n\nInstall the package:\n\n```bash\ngo get github.com/jackc/pgx/v5/pgxpool\n```\n\nCreate the pool:\n\n```go\nconfig, err := pgxpool.ParseConfig(\"postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/mydb\")\nif err != nil {\n    log.Fatal(err)\n}\nconfig.MaxConns = 10\nconfig.MinConns = 2\n\npool, err := pgxpool.NewWithConfig(context.Background(), config)\nif err != nil {\n    log.Fatal(err)\n}\ndefer pool.Close()\n```\n\n`MaxConns` caps the total number of open connections. Under heavy load, goroutines block on `pool.Acquire()` once this limit is hit, so size it based on your expected concurrency and what your PostgreSQL server can handle. `MinConns` keeps idle connections warm so the first few queries after a quiet period don't pay the connection setup cost.\n\nThe pool checks connection health automatically and reconnects when a connection goes stale. You don't need to handle this yourself."}}], "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 624, "cache_read_input_tokens": 39161, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 624, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 1}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "a0aff4c2-5f9d-4475-9715-42925acbb5ef", "uuid": "cb53b886-d80c-4a09-9462-b984188e8386", "timestamp": "2026-05-04T07:52:20.681Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01YKKcQg1foD32x1fquQ9uje","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /private/var/folders/3v/16m6pn090hl4d60grm1057ww0000gn/T/agent-eval/2026-05-04-opus/cases/case-004-tutorial-with-code/source.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0aff4c2-5f9d-4475-9715-42925acbb5ef","uuid":"e394b3a4-409d-4a3d-bb95-27ccf5965b06","timestamp":"2026-05-04T07:52:20.742Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/3v/16m6pn090hl4d60grm1057ww0000gn/T/agent-eval/2026-05-04-opus/cases/case-004-tutorial-with-code/source.md","oldString":"## Setting Up A PostgreSQL Connection Pool\n\nIn order to establish a connection pool in your Go application, you'll need to leverage the `pgxpool` package. This groundbreaking library serves as a robust foundation for managing database connections efficiently.\n\nFirst, install the package:\n\n```bash\ngo get github.com/jackc/pgx/v5/pgxpool\n```\n\nNext, create the pool configuration. The following code showcases how to set up a connection pool with sensible defaults:\n\n```go\nconfig, err := pgxpool.ParseConfig(\"postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/mydb\")\nif err != nil {\n    log.Fatal(err)\n}\nconfig.MaxConns = 10\nconfig.MinConns = 2\n\npool, err := pgxpool.NewWithConfig(context.Background(), config)\nif err != nil {\n    log.Fatal(err)\n}\ndefer pool.Close()\n```\n\nIt is important to note that the `MaxConns` parameter plays a crucial role in determining the pool's behavior under load. Additionally, the `MinConns` setting ensures that a minimum number of connections are maintained, fostering improved response times for initial queries.\n\nThe pool boasts automatic connection health checking and reconnection capabilities. Due to the fact that connections can become stale, the pool periodically validates them, highlighting the intricate interplay between resource management and reliability.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you'd like me to delve into connection lifecycle management.","newString":"## Setting up a PostgreSQL connection pool\n\nThe `pgxpool` package manages connection pooling for Go applications using PostgreSQL.\n\nInstall the package:\n\n```bash\ngo get github.com/jackc/pgx/v5/pgxpool\n```\n\nCreate the pool:\n\n```go\nconfig, err := pgxpool.ParseConfig(\"postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/mydb\")\nif err != nil {\n    log.Fatal(err)\n}\nconfig.MaxConns = 10\nconfig.MinConns = 2\n\npool, err := pgxpool.NewWithConfig(context.Background(), config)\nif err != nil {\n    log.Fatal(err)\n}\ndefer pool.Close()\n```\n\n`MaxConns` caps the total number of open connections. Under heavy load, goroutines block on `pool.Acquire()` once this limit is hit, so size it based on your expected concurrency and what your PostgreSQL server can handle. `MinConns` keeps idle connections warm so the first few queries after a quiet period don't pay the connection setup cost.\n\nThe pool checks connection health automatically and reconnects when a connection goes stale. You don't need to handle this yourself.","originalFile":"## Setting Up A PostgreSQL Connection Pool\n\nIn order to establish a connection pool in your Go application, you'll need to leverage the `pgxpool` package. This groundbreaking library serves as a robust foundation for managing database connections efficiently.\n\nFirst, install the package:\n\n```bash\ngo get github.com/jackc/pgx/v5/pgxpool\n```\n\nNext, create the pool configuration. The following code showcases how to set up a connection pool with sensible defaults:\n\n```go\nconfig, err := pgxpool.ParseConfig(\"postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/mydb\")\nif err != nil {\n    log.Fatal(err)\n}\nconfig.MaxConns = 10\nconfig.MinConns = 2\n\npool, err := pgxpool.NewWithConfig(context.Background(), config)\nif err != nil {\n    log.Fatal(err)\n}\ndefer pool.Close()\n```\n\nIt is important to note that the `MaxConns` parameter plays a crucial role in determining the pool's behavior under load. Additionally, the `MinConns` setting ensures that a minimum number of connections are maintained, fostering improved response times for initial queries.\n\nThe pool boasts automatic connection health checking and reconnection capabilities. Due to the fact that connections can become stale, the pool periodically validates them, highlighting the intricate interplay between resource management and reliability.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you'd like me to delve into connection lifecycle management.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":14,"newStart":1,"newLines":14,"lines":["-## Setting Up A PostgreSQL Connection Pool","+## Setting up a PostgreSQL connection pool"," ","-In order to establish a connection pool in your Go application, you'll need to leverage the `pgxpool` package. This groundbreaking library serves as a robust foundation for managing database connections efficiently.","+The `pgxpool` package manages connection pooling for Go applications using PostgreSQL."," ","-First, install the package:","+Install the package:"," "," ```bash"," go get github.com/jackc/pgx/v5/pgxpool"," ```"," ","-Next, create the pool configuration. The following code showcases how to set up a connection pool with sensible defaults:","+Create the pool:"," "," ```go"," config, err := pgxpool.ParseConfig(\"postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/mydb\")"]},{"oldStart":25,"oldLines":8,"newStart":25,"newLines":6,"lines":[" defer pool.Close()"," ```"," ","-It is important to note that the `MaxConns` parameter plays a crucial role in determining the pool's behavior under load. Additionally, the `MinConns` setting ensures that a minimum number of connections are maintained, fostering improved response times for initial queries.","+`MaxConns` caps the total number of open connections. Under heavy load, goroutines block on `pool.Acquire()` once this limit is hit, so size it based on your expected concurrency and what your PostgreSQL server can handle. `MinConns` keeps idle connections warm so the first few queries after a quiet period don't pay the connection setup cost."," ","-The pool boasts automatic connection health checking and reconnection capabilities. Due to the fact that connections can become stale, the pool periodically validates them, highlighting the intricate interplay between resource management and reliability.","-","-I hope this helps! Let me know if you'd like me to delve into connection lifecycle management.","+The pool checks connection health automatically and reconnects when a connection goes stale. You don't need to handle this yourself."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
